#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105386 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "9fa21896-8127-5ec5-aff0-7f811403db8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12442" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-1,7)$ and parallel to $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105387 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points"}] :question/id #uuid "d1adb6fb-cd44-5a12-b892-6a6d2c99929d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12443" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,7⟩-⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105388 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"}] :question/id #uuid "55ee3b10-087f-521e-ba37-12541e199408" :question/math-academy-id "e-12444" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(1,2,0)$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}= \\langle -4,1,1 \\rangle.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $PA=6\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩)-⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=6\\sqrt{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PA & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{18{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\pm 3t\\sqrt{2} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨1,2,0⟩+2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨-7,4,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨1,2,0⟩-2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨9,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105389 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/id #uuid "69c3fc44-8b4d-521e-b207-0ea01d5921e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12445" :question/problem "Using $\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j},\\mathbf{k}$ notation, find a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(-4,1,2)$ and $Q(-2,5,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+5\\,j-k)-(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{r} = (-4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}) + t (2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\, \\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(4t+1)\\,j+(2-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4da8e952-8593-578a-9d48-203d6671854e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨4,1,6⟩+⟨3,-2,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311099} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fcdc2e-b476-5768-ad2f-3ad7b05e8845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨6,4,1⟩+t⟨3,-2,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311100} {:answer/id #uuid "133ba8a0-3b4b-5de7-9e2d-ee9b3aacb175" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨5,-2,3⟩+t⟨6,4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311101} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8b9d92-2608-51cc-a4dd-42a42eeaa800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-2,5⟩+t⟨4,1,6⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311102} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1d8308-0e4b-5426-a11b-b5e72aac68c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨4,1,6⟩+t⟨3,-2,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f8b9d92-2608-51cc-a4dd-42a42eeaa800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-2,5⟩+t⟨4,1,6⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311102} :answer-field/id #uuid "416b1888-8a3e-5af1-8c28-1698c29ae483" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6f778f2-0d6a-54b4-923f-915d79d5f301" :question/math-academy-id "q-126430" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(3,-2,5)$ and parallel to $v=⟨4,1,6⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨3,-2,5⟩.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨3,-2,5⟩+t⟨4,1,6⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edb95343-d14b-5c9d-a0f9-c4b57323a384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311106} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed5d913-6b6c-5367-8541-030232b01964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311107} {:answer/id #uuid "46105c4e-45f2-5924-bb5a-e89dd6113e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311108} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8758bc-6709-57c9-9a62-d574605c3516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311109} {:answer/id #uuid "1871cc37-f619-5953-91eb-dcfa6e561709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ed5d913-6b6c-5367-8541-030232b01964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311107} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7d8119a-200e-536b-a008-1a1710b3a4d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aaf48b17-9dee-583c-9e29-c168f66ed80b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126458" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $C(-1,0,4)$ and $D(-7,2,11).$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $C$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=c+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $c$ is the position vector of $C.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $C(-1,0,4)$ and $D(-7,2,11)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =⟨-7,2,11⟩-⟨-1,0,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,2,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb34a150-e343-5f14-b288-386875381a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,2,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(-5,3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311113} {:answer/id #uuid "c8456756-54ca-592c-a289-99edc3aa3d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,-5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311114} {:answer/id #uuid "a406d08d-e6bb-5c61-8aad-1593b1da44c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,1,-5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311115} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9c966a-5aed-50c4-b30e-c9cc186fe8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311116} {:answer/id #uuid "641b04fa-ffe5-5154-b59c-31c024713831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(4,-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c9c966a-5aed-50c4-b30e-c9cc186fe8a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311116} :answer-field/id #uuid "34a23037-a100-5f54-85a7-3c0b9303e5a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cbeb3212-31dc-5cc0-971a-bf4846f229e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-126992" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,-2,3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-2,1,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,-2,3⟩+t⟨-2,1,2⟩)-⟨3,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\,t,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=3,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =3 \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =3 \\\\ |⟨-2t,\\,t,\\,2t⟩| & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}} & =3 \\\\ \\pm 3t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,-2,3⟩+⟨-2,1,2⟩=⟨1,-1,5⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,-2,3⟩-⟨-2,1,2⟩=⟨5,-3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(1,-1,5)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-3,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9a26130-b9ff-57cc-bb11-c79826a6b802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,-1,-7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311120} {:answer/id #uuid "52dede04-bba1-5c39-a410-2592c55d3b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311121} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e62b70-6be7-5357-9bca-77c48e37e961" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,-1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311122} {:answer/id #uuid "f3804c30-94da-5f7a-b59b-deffb0ce5f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,-5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311123} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb5cfbc-22c3-528a-a30a-5c064ab2aa50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,-7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52dede04-bba1-5c39-a410-2592c55d3b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311121} :answer-field/id #uuid "767e4e2d-0457-5fea-a1d0-540a9ba8e186" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59a52ddc-339d-53d5-a5c4-8b8787ceb738" :question/math-academy-id "q-126995" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(2,3,6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-3,-2,1⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨2,3,6⟩+t⟨-3,-2,1⟩)-⟨2,3,6⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-3,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨-3t,\\,-2t,\\,t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-3t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨2,3,6⟩+⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨-1,1,7⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨-3,-2,1⟩=⟨5,5,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(-1,1,7)$ and ${A}_{2}(5,5,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce24760-9e5a-579a-bf02-dc5d8c8762c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311127} {:answer/id #uuid "84296384-e8d0-558e-a49f-64e18e2954cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311128} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcf0c16-abc6-53e6-84d8-27e642ba913c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311129} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b34324-ccf2-5c5d-aa4f-7ececfba962f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311130} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd7e1cf-d4ed-56f3-8404-8d355d7c274a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dd7e1cf-d4ed-56f3-8404-8d355d7c274a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311131} :answer-field/id #uuid "e38cf0b5-6047-52e7-aa8a-656fc066d8dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fedc490-872a-5bf2-a731-f24282a97e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162168" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-6,2,-1)$ and $Q(-8,0,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-1)+(-2)+1=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85617a24-64b7-5da1-9338-83bce6bbcd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311134} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0147ca-bcd1-583b-ad66-678a590c7e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311135} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3e2f1d-ced2-5713-977d-d3119e1b264e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311136} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc57ed6-6e6e-51ee-95b3-3a3319fe89a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311137} {:answer/id #uuid "c57adfc5-5ef8-51f2-8585-960b427e6ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85617a24-64b7-5da1-9338-83bce6bbcd63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311134} :answer-field/id #uuid "40261d74-6aea-550b-97b3-972cdc7008f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12c5ba63-0750-5dc9-b617-46b3345e0ebe" :question/math-academy-id "q-162169" :question/problem "The equation below describes all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(5,0,4)$ and $Q(-3,1,5).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ a \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+1+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c629dade-033a-586a-86cb-9a3a28ab2c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311141} {:answer/id #uuid "5bba45e5-faf5-5659-93d3-53ec7bf78a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311142} {:answer/id #uuid "9a730777-44f1-53e9-a66a-0de5f90d7a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311143} {:answer/id #uuid "711bc41e-b8e6-5954-ab38-2933d27f8321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311144} {:answer/id #uuid "a315c075-de13-5d23-9919-27d8cb3fba4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c629dade-033a-586a-86cb-9a3a28ab2c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311141} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6a4c70d-05f1-57b6-b794-8282742733bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7a17e80-b445-5fbb-9914-628d51dd5f0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162173" :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(3,2,-1)$ and $Q(0,1,-3).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=a\\,i+b\\,j-(2t+1)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(j-3\\,k)-(3\\,i+2\\,j-k) \\\\  & =-3\\,i-j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(3\\,i+2\\,j-k)+t(-3\\,i-j-2\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(3\\,i+2\\,j-k)+t(-3\\,i-j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =(3-3t)\\,i+(2-t)\\,j-(2t+1)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(3-3t)+(2-t)+1=6-4t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b176559-03f2-57f1-83fe-717bbc3e1046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311148} {:answer/id #uuid "5749ad62-7506-58fa-a881-afe808e03b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311149} {:answer/id #uuid "a66c7d92-05c4-5558-8fdc-1596446f8d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311150} {:answer/id #uuid "62af6b74-b1fb-5d95-a048-3bda6a9b9eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311151} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba5c6f6-469a-5d69-8f13-cb408e46e9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a66c7d92-05c4-5558-8fdc-1596446f8d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311150} :answer-field/id #uuid "52ed265e-d3be-558b-95ca-48d2fe2d4d0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebfee70e-e836-552c-939d-eb60ebe536a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162177" :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-1,1,3)$ and $Q(0,6,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=a\\,i+(5t+1)\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(6\\,j-k)-(-i+j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =i+5\\,j-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(-i+j+3\\,k)+t(i+5\\,j-4\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-i+j+3\\,k)+t(i+5\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =(t-1)\\,i+(5t+1)\\,j+(3-4t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(t-1)+(3-4t)+0=2-3t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee4e9b21-a88f-5640-b301-5f5a2b519442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311155} {:answer/id #uuid "b20d91a1-6b28-50c3-9033-8b514118ebcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311156} {:answer/id #uuid "49d5d941-8d26-5a14-b4c5-3fd9b43ff98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311157} {:answer/id #uuid "60239240-08ee-55f4-bc03-d8b8444d03f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311158} {:answer/id #uuid "254171cb-3f76-5317-9a4d-cd0e6615d80a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20d91a1-6b28-50c3-9033-8b514118ebcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311156} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b295282-e9c1-539c-b812-6e1a9ce491c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf4eef75-2e3f-541f-82d8-e3782a41d018" :question/math-academy-id "q-162184" :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-4,0,-3)$ and $Q(-6,2,-5).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=-a\\,i+2t\\,j-b\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(-6\\,i+2\\,j-5\\,k)-(-4\\,i-3\\,k) \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(-4\\,i-3\\,k)+t(-2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-4\\,i-3\\,k)+t(-2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-(2t+4)\\,i+2t\\,j-(2t+3)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(2t+4)+(2t+3)+1=4t+8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8da153c-bf9b-5c62-a868-4ac4c412c3f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨8,0,-5⟩+t⟨1,-2,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311162} {:answer/id #uuid "869f5fa5-7eeb-5810-a821-37cc24da945a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨8,0,-5⟩+t⟨-2,1,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311163} {:answer/id #uuid "60cef0df-db2c-5c14-9a00-508453d54cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,8,-2⟩+t⟨8,-5,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311164} {:answer/id #uuid "437779bb-cf02-5e5c-bca8-adfa82b09af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-2,1,8⟩+⟨8,0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311165} {:answer/id #uuid "c9497be1-d0fc-5981-825e-f709c42f352d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-2,1,8⟩+t⟨8,0,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9497be1-d0fc-5981-825e-f709c42f352d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-2,1,8⟩+t⟨8,0,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311166} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a663f52-f8cb-54bd-ad09-e2ae35a91dae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a298fdc-2b4e-5d13-9ff1-366f2f62e2b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-22285" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(-2,1,8)$ and parallel to $v=⟨8,0,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,1,8⟩.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨-2,1,8⟩+t⟨8,0,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points on a Line That Are a Fixed Distance From Another Point on the Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892051aa-0877-50b9-8675-d6147aaf2167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A_{1}(4, 2, 2)$ and $A_{2}(1, 0,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102946} {:answer/id #uuid "0522dc2f-cf3c-5300-865e-d202d312457d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A_{1}(2, 5, 2)$ and $A_{2}(0, 1,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102947} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0a7363-0b7f-53ae-9178-ceca0c1bc52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A_{1}(5, 2, 2)$ and $A_{2}(1, 0,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102948} {:answer/id #uuid "30c56214-2918-5ac9-9360-cf8ed1d4cf21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A_{1}(5, 2, 3)$ and $A_{2}(1,-1,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102949} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0e94ce-4733-56f2-98cd-7581aba577f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A_{1}(5, 3, 2)$ and $A_{2}(1, 0, 4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a0a7363-0b7f-53ae-9178-ceca0c1bc52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A_{1}(5, 2, 2)$ and $A_{2}(1, 0,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102948} :answer-field/id #uuid "697e8978-86f9-5917-be7c-401361a05ea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f95ebb39-0273-5ef9-b622-24560965b7f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-22291" :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,1,3⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩)-⟨3,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩+1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨5,2,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩-1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨1,0,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(5,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,0,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"}] :question/an